Fillets: the walk round the vertex at a rounding's end past a short edge
of that vertex (mitcad#236).

PerformIntersectionAtEnd walks from the contact's edge round a face of
the stripe to the vertex and takes, at each vertex passed, the face across
the next edge and the common edge of that face and the one before. Where
the walk passes a short edge of the vertex (an edge of 13 micrometres
between the corner's wall and the face beyond, below which the wall meets
a rim's torus), that common edge does not hold the vertex.

- The common edge of two faces was the first one found: two faces may
  share several edges (a torus and a cylinder meeting along both ends of
  a groove, a line broken by a notch), and the far one was taken; the
  corner then failed ("pb orientation"). It is now the common edge at the
  vertex where the walk stands (any one where none passes that vertex).
- Where that edge is prolonged along the intersection of its faces'
  surfaces (a B-spline edge whose own curve ends at the edge), the
  prolongation started at the corner's vertex, which does not lie on that
  intersection: the result had self-intersecting wires. It now starts at
  the vertex where the walk reached the edge, and the short edge lies in
  the removed corner (as 0071's prolongation from an edge's end does).

Upstream: new.
